\description{
The design twin of \code{\link[=table_categorical]{table_categorical()}}: counts and estimated
percentages of categorical variables computed from a
\code{survey::svydesign()} or \code{survey::as.svrepdesign()} object instead
of a data frame.

Every statistic is survey's. \code{survey::svymean()} estimates the
percentages and their design effects, \code{survey::svyciprop()} their
confidence intervals, and \code{survey::svychisq()} tests the
association, Rao-Scott corrected and referred to the design degrees
of freedom.
}
\section{What the columns are}{
\code{n} is the \emph{observed} count -- the number of rows in the sample, not
an estimated population size. \verb{\%} is the estimated percentage
\emph{within} its column: without \code{by} it is the distribution of the
variable in the population, with \code{by} the distribution inside that
domain. The table note gives the sample size and the estimated
population together, because neither alone tells the reader what
they are looking at.

\code{proportion_ci = TRUE} adds the interval of each percentage.
\code{ci_method} chooses among the seven \code{survey::svyciprop()} offers;
the default \code{"logit"} is bounded inside 0 to 100, which the Wald
interval (\code{"mean"}) is not. The percentage itself always comes from
\code{survey::svymean()}, so it does not move when \code{ci_method} does.

A percentage of 0 or 100 has no design-based interval: its estimated
variance is zero. Whatever \code{ci_method}, such a cell gets the exact
binomial (Clopper-Pearson) interval on the unweighted count of its
column, with no degrees-of-freedom factor. This is the rule of Korn
and Graubard (1998, p. 194) for an estimate of 0, which the NCHS data
presentation standards for proportions apply to its complement as well
(Parker et al. 2017, pp. 2-3). The note says so when such a cell is
shown.
}

\section{The test}{
\code{svychisq()} with \code{chisq_statistic = "F"} (the default) is the
Pearson chi-square with the Rao-Scott second-order correction,
referred to F(ndf, \code{survey::degf(design)}). It is survey's own
default and the one Stata's \code{svy: tabulate} reports. The design-based
test has no exact counterpart, so the switch to Fisher's exact test
that \code{test} makes in \code{\link[=table_categorical]{table_categorical()}} when an expected count is
below 5 has no equivalent here.

It runs on the \emph{complete cases} of the two variables, and on their
observed levels: a \code{(Missing)} row and a declared-but-unobserved
level are descriptive, and neither belongs to the null hypothesis.
The p-value is therefore the same whether \code{drop_na} shows those rows
or removes them, and the intervals beside it describe the same
domain -- the two families test the same table.

\code{"Chisq"} shows the p-value only: survey adjusts the statistic in
the \code{"F"} branch and only the p-value in the \code{"Chisq"} one, so the
statistic there is not the one the p-value came from. \code{"Wald"},
\code{"adjWald"} and \code{"saddlepoint"} are available; \code{"lincom"} and
\code{"wls-score"} are refused, the first because its integration is
documented as failing in the far tail (\code{?pchisqsum}), the second
because it has no reporting convention here.
}

\section{Groups with no data}{
As in \code{\link[=table_categorical]{table_categorical()}}, a variable with no observed value in a
group of \code{by} that holds sampled rows, typically a question not asked
in that group, has no data there, which is not a missing answer. Its
cells in that group show an en dash (\code{NA} in the data frames, in
\link[=tidy.spicy_categorical_svy_table]{tidy()} and in \code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}}),
and its rows there are left out of everything computed for that
variable: the missing values the note counts, the \code{Total} block, the
\code{(Missing)} row and the test. A variable left with fewer than two
groups with data is not tested: its \code{p} cell is left blank. The note
says "No data for X in G.", in one sentence for the variables with no
data in the same groups ("No data for X and Y in G."). Without \code{by}, a
variable with no observed value at all keeps its declared levels (its
one row when it declares none) with the dash, and the note says "No
data for X."
}

\section{Stability}{
This function is \strong{stabilizing} in the sense \code{?spicy} defines: the
names of its design-specific arguments may still be tightened
before 1.0 -- with a \code{NEWS.md} entry -- but the behavior does not
change silently. It was experimental through 0.13; the shape of the
table has held across the cycle, so it now moves on the parent
family's clock rather than its own. The numbers themselves are
survey's and do not move with it.
}

\section{What is absent, and why}{
\code{weights} and \code{rescale} (the weighting \emph{is} the design). \code{correct}
(Yates), \code{simulate_p} and \code{simulate_B}, which have no meaning once
the reference distribution is Rao-Scott's. And the association
measures: Cramer's V, phi, tau-b/c, gamma, Somers' D and lambda
have no established design-based variance, and the intervals
\code{\link[=table_categorical]{table_categorical()}} gives them assume simple random sampling. The
design-based measure of association here is the Rao-Scott test in
the \code{p} column; for an effect size, model it with
\code{table_regression(survey::svyglm(...))}.
}

\section{Multiple-comparison adjustment}{
The p-values of a descriptive table describe: they test no
prespecified hypothesis. STROBE (explanation of item 14) and the
SAMPL guidelines advise against significance tests in descriptive and
baseline tables, hence the default \code{p_adjust = "none"}.

When a journal or a protocol asks for adjusted p-values anyway,
\code{p_adjust} applies \code{\link[stats:p.adjust]{stats::p.adjust()}} to the family of every test in
the table: one p-value per variable (one per block in
\code{\link[=table_outcome]{table_outcome()}}), never the rows of one variable, which share one
test. \code{"holm"} suits a confirmatory set of comparisons, \code{"BH"} a
screening one; \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}} lists the methods. A test that
returned no p-value stays out of the family.

The adjustment runs before any formatting, so the \code{p} column and its
\verb{<.001} threshold show the adjusted values, and the table note gives
the number of tests and the method. The raw p-values stay in the
object: a \code{p_unadjusted} column next to the p-value column of the
returned data frames, and \code{col_meta$p$p_unadjusted} (with the method
in \code{p_adjust} and the family size in \code{p_adjust_m}) in
\code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}}. Without \code{by}, or with \code{p_value = FALSE}, the table
shows no p-value to adjust, and a \code{p_adjust} other than \code{"none"} is an
error.
}

